speechtext:
Standing order 48 reads:
When a question is under debate no motion is received unless to amend it; to postpone it to a day certain; for the previous question; for reading the orders of the day;
If the orders of the day are read, it closes the debate.
for proceeding to another order;
That is a motion of closure.
to adjourn the debate;
That is also closure.
or for the adjournment of the house.
That is closure. It is not in my power to apply closure, but there it is in the standing orders as adopted in 1913. A motion to commit can be made at any stage of the bill, after the second reading or even upon the first reading. Standing order 50 states that it is not amendable, and standing order 38 does not enumerate a motion to commit among the debatable motions. It is an ancillary motion and no notice is required for such motion.
